<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-2-6-errors">
  <time minutes="45" />
  <title>Errors</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Debugging is a normal part of coding. It can be frustrating at times, but you will get
      better at it with practice! Watch the following video to see that all coders get bugs.
    </p>

    <video youtube="auv10y-dN4s" label="video-debugging" />
    
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="correcting-errors">
    <title>Correcting Errors</title>

    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Errors are a normal part of programming. As programmers develop code, they repeatedly 
        encounter errors, identify their cause, and revise the program to correct them.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>One of the first documented computer bugs was discovered by an early
      computer science pioneer <url
      href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace_Hopper">Grace Hopper</url>, and it was a moth that flew into 
      a panel of a large computer in 1947! Grace Hopper debugged the computer
     and pasted the actual bug in her logbook.
    </p>

    <figure align="left">
      <caption>Grace Hopper’s log showing a real bug, 1947.</caption>
      <image source="_static/Unit2images/firstbug.jpg" width="54%" />
    </figure>


    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Programmers often discover errors through user testing or feedback, such as when users 
        receive confusing messages, see inconsistent results, or cannot complete a task. Programmers 
        use observations of what the user did and what the app displayed to clarify what the behavior 
        should be for the user. To fix the error, they try to reproduce it by following the same user 
        actions and inputs, which helps confirm the cause and verify that the correction solves the 
        real user problem.
      </p>
    </note>

     <p>Everyone who codes encounters bugs or errors in their programs. Sometimes the user of your program will report
      the problem and the programmer will have to figure out what went wrong. 
     </p>
    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Knowing common types of errors helps programmers diagnose problems more quickly and choose 
        effective strategies to fix them when they occur.
      </p>
    </note>

       <idx>syntax error</idx>

     <note>
      <title>2.6.A.5</title>
      <p>
        A <term>syntax error</term> occurs when the code breaks the rules of the programming 
        language, preventing the program from running as written.
      </p>
    </note>

    
    <p>When you are coding, the most common type of error is a <term>syntax error</term>, which means that you
      didn't write the Python code correctly. For example, if you forget to close a parenthesis or use the wrong indentation, 
      you will get a syntax error reported by the Python interpreter.</p>

     <p>
      The Python interpreter tries to run your code, but if your code has <term>syntax
      errors</term>, you will see error messages displayed below the code.
     The error messages will tell the line number of the error and the type of error. 
     The error messages are not always easy to understand and sometimes the actual error is before the line reported, but
     that is where the interpreter crashed on your code. Try to debug the code below. Read the error messages underneath to help you. 
     Here are some common syntax errors to look for:
    </p>
     <ul>
        <li>Check for missing or extra parentheses, brackets, or quotation marks.</li>
        <li>Check for missing colons at the end of a line that starts a new block of code.</li>
        <li>Check for incorrect indentation.</li>
        <li>Check for misspelled keywords or variable names.</li>
      </ul>
    

     <note>
      <title>2.6.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Programmers use error messages to identify and correct problems because they describe what 
        went wrong and often point to where it occurred. They may include the type of error, such as 
        syntax or runtime, the line or location in the code, and details about the operation or value 
        that caused the problem. Programmers use error messages to decide what to check first, make a 
        targeted change, and then rerun the program to confirm the error is resolved.
      </p>
    </note>

    <activity label="debug-syntax-error">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Debug the code below. Read the error messages underneath to help you.
        </p>
      </statement>

        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
 Print("Hello World
      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("Hello world",self.getOutput(), "Check output")
              
          myTests().main()
      </tests>
     </program>
    </activity>

      <idx>logic error</idx>

      <note>
      <title>2.6.A.4</title>
      <p>
        A <term>logic error</term> occurs when the program runs but produces incorrect or unexpected 
        behavior because the algorithm or reasoning is incorrect.
      </p>
    </note>

   <p>
      Some errors cannot be detected by the interpreter. These are called <term>logic errors</term> 
      or <term>run-time errors</term>. 
      Logic errors occur when the program runs, but the output is not what you expected. For example,
      a programmer might have made a math mistake so the wrong value is
      calculated. Logic errors can be detected by testing the program with
      specific data to see if it produces the expected outcome.
    </p> 

    <activity label="debug-logic-error">
      <statement>
        <p>
         The following code has a logic error. It is supposed to calculate the average of three scores.
         Try running it and you will see that it does not give the correct answer. 
         Fix the math operator in the code to correct the logic error and run to test.
        </p>
      </statement>

        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
score1 = 100
score2 = 95
score3 = 90
average = (score1 + score2 + score3) * 3
print("The average score is", average)
      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("95",self.getOutput(), "The average should be 95")
              self.assertIn("/",self.getEditorText(), "Use / instead of * to calculate the average")
              
          myTests().main()
      </tests>
     </program>
    </activity>

        <idx>runtime error</idx>

    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.6</title>
      <p>
        A <term>runtime error</term> occurs while a program is executing when it encounters a 
        situation it cannot handle, such as dividing by zero. Runtime errors may cause a program to 
        stop running unless the error is handled. Programming languages define the specific runtime 
        errors they produce.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      <term>Run-time errors</term> occur when the program is running by something unexpected, such as dividing by zero or 
      trying to read from a file that doesn’t exist.
    </p>
   
    <activity label="debug-runtime-error">
      <statement>
        <p>
         Run the following code to see the run-time error of dividing by zero (which is not allowed). 
         Fix the code to correct the run-time error and run to test.
        </p>
      </statement>

        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
score1 = 100
score2 = 95
score3 = 90
average = (score1 + score2 + score3) / 0
print("The average score is", average)
      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("95",self.getOutput(), "The average should be 95")
              self.assertIn("3",self.getEditorText(), "Divide by 3 instead of 0 to calculate the average")
              
          myTests().main()
      </tests>
     </program>
     </activity>

    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.8</title>
      <p>
        Programmers trace a program's execution to determine how variable values change and which 
        statements execute. They may trace manually or use debugging tools that step through code and 
        display current variable values. Tracing helps identify where a program's behavior differs 
        from what was expected and is especially useful for finding logic errors. It can also be used 
        to determine what conditions led to a runtime error.
      </p>
    </note>

      <p>Tracing is a great way to debug code, especially with logic or runtime errors.
        To trace code, pretend you are the computer and go through each line of code step by step,
        keeping track of the values of variables as you go. The Code Lens button in the active code exercises
       can help you to debug your code. It is based on the <url href="https://pythontutor.com/">Python Tutor</url> website.
       Try using the Code Lens button or the Python Tutor tool to trace the code in the debugging exercises above.
      </p>
    

    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.9</title>
      <p>
        Programmers can add output statements to temporarily display intermediate values or messages 
        while a program runs, such as printing variable values at key points. This helps confirm 
        which parts of the code are executing and whether variables and conditions have the expected 
        values. By comparing the observed output to the expected output, programmers can narrow down 
        where the program's behavior first begins to differ from what was expected and identify the 
        source of an error.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>Another way to keep track of what is happening in your code is to add print statements to display the values of variables 
      at key points in the program. Try it below. 
    </p>
    <activity label="debug-with-print">
      <statement>
        <p>
         The following code has a logic error. The loop is supposed to total the numbers 0 through 5, but it only adds 0 through 4. 
         Add a print statement inside the loop to display the value of the variable number at each iteration. 
         Then run the code to see what is happening and fix the logic error.
        </p>
      </statement>

        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        total = 0
        for number in range(5):
            # Add print number to help you to debug the code
            total += number

        print("Total:", total)
      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("15",self.getOutput(), "The total should be 15")
              self.assertIn("range(6)",self.getEditorText(), "Change the range to 6 to include 5 in the total")
              self.assertIn("print(number)",self.getEditorText(), "Add print(number) in the loop")

          myTests().main()
      </tests>
      </program>
    </activity
    >
    <note>
      <title>2.6.A.10</title>
      <p>
        AI tools can support programmers in interpreting error messages, explaining what a piece of 
        code is doing, and suggesting possible causes and fixes for unexpected behavior. Programmers 
        can describe the expected vs. actual output and share relevant code so the tool can point out 
        likely logic mistakes or recommend ways of making the code easier to trace. AI suggestions 
        should be verified by running the program, checking results against requirements, and using 
        other debugging methods, since AI-generated explanations or fixes may be incomplete or 
        incorrect.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>If you get stuck trying to find the bugs, sometimes another pair of eyes really helps. 
      Ask a friend if you get stuck or try explaining your code line by line
      to someone or even a rubber duck. <url
      href="https://rubberduckdebugging.com/">Rubber duck debugging</url> is a
      lot of fun! This site also has a virtual rubber duck AI tool that can help you. 
      You can also use AI tools to help you debug your code, but remember that the AI tool may not always be correct, 
      so you should always check the suggestions and test your code.
    </p>
     <image src="_static/Unit2images/rubberduck.jpg" alt="Rubber duck debugging" />
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Testing and Test Cases</title>
    <idx>test cases</idx>
    <note>
      <title>2.6.B.1</title>
      <p>
        In the development process, testing uses defined inputs to check whether an algorithm or 
        program produces the expected outcomes. Programmers compare actual results to expected 
        results and use what they learn to revise the algorithm or program. A <term>test case</term> 
        consists of a specific input and the expected output or behavior that should result from that input.  
        AI tools can help suggest test cases and predict expected results, but programmers are 
        responsible for validating expected outcomes and using test results to confirm correctness.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.6.B.2</title>
      <p>
        Programmers often derive test cases from user stories and user testing by translating real 
        user tasks into inputs and expected outcomes, including what the user should see at each 
        step. This helps ensure the algorithm is correct in ways that matter to users.
      </p>
    </note>

    <idx>test plan</idx>

    <p>When testing code, programmers create test cases with specific inputs and expected outputs. They run the code with the test case  inputs and compare the actual output to the expected output.
      If the actual output does not match the expected output, then there is a bug in the code that needs to be fixed. 
      A test plan with a table like the one below can be used to record the testing results for a program.
       <table xml:id="test-cases">
      <title>Test Cases</title>
        <tabular>
             <row header="yes">
                <cell bottom="minor"> Test Case</cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Input </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Expected Output </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Actual Output </cell>
            </row>  
            </tabular>
      </table>
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>2.6.B.3</title>
      <p>
        An effective set of test cases includes inputs that produce different expected outputs or 
        behaviors, including typical, boundary, and invalid cases. <term>Boundary test cases</term> use values at 
        or near important limits, such as the minimum and maximum allowed inputs or decision 
        thresholds in conditional expressions. <term>Invalid test cases</term> may use values just beyond the 
        allowed range to check how the program handles likely user mistakes.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>In code with selection statements, it is important to test each branch of the conditional to ensure that the program behaves correctly for all possible inputs. With loops, it is important to use <term>boundary test cases</term> to test the loop's behavior at the limits of its range. Invalid test cases with inputs just beyond the limits of the conditions for selection or loops should also be tested.</p>

        <note>
      <title>2.6.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Some algorithms appear correct for common inputs but fail for other inputs, so programmers 
        test multiple cases that cover different paths through the algorithm, such as different 
        branches of a conditional or different numbers of loop repetitions. AI tools can help 
        identify distinct execution paths and suggest test cases for each, but programmers should 
        confirm that the suggested tests actually reach those paths.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.6.B.5</title>
      <p>
        Testing reflects real users: people mistype, submit blanks, use unexpected values, click 
        repeatedly, or do steps out of order. Including these inputs as test cases helps ensure the 
        app or program responds clearly and consistently, including giving useful feedback when input 
        is invalid.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="POGIL-test-plans">
    <title>POGIL Test Plans</title>
   
     <idx><h>POGIL group activity</h><h>test plans</h></idx>
     <idx><h>projects</h><h>test plans</h></idx>
     
    <p>Work in POGIL groups to test the following programs with test plans (<url href="https://pogil.org/what-is-pogil">What is POGIL</url>, 
            <url href="https://pogil.org/uploads/attachments/wcuuolutqjpvkazq12dv7kfl-pogillaminatedrolecards-final.pdf">POGIL Role Cards</url>).
   </p> 
         
    <p>Try the code below which has some logic errors. Use the test plan in the table in the activity below to test it. After you have filled in the actual output column, you can fix the code
          by changing "if" to "elif" after the first "if" to chain all the conditions together.
          This would be a good exercise to do in pairs or POGIL groups.</p>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
score = int(input("Enter a score: "))
if score &gt;= 90:
    grade = "A"
if score &gt;= 80:
    grade = "B"
if score &gt;= 70:
    grade = "C"
if score &gt;= 60:
    grade = "D"
else:
    grade = "F"
print(grade)
        </code>
      </program>

    <activity label="selection-test-cases">
      <title>Selection Test Cases</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Run the code above following the test plan below. Fill in the actual output column of the table.
          This code has some logic errors. 
          <table>
        <tabular>
             <row header="yes">
                <cell bottom="minor">Test Case</cell>
                <cell bottom="minor">Input </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor">Expected Output </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor">Actual Output </cell>
            </row>  
            <row>
                <cell>Grade A</cell>
                <cell>90</cell>
                <cell>A</cell>
                <cell> <fillin answer="D" mode="string" /></cell>
            </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Grade B</cell>
                <cell>80</cell>
                <cell>B</cell>
                <cell> <fillin answer="D" mode="string" /></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Grade C</cell>
                <cell>70</cell>
                <cell>C</cell>
                <cell> <fillin answer="D" mode="string" /></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Grade D</cell>
                <cell>60</cell>
                <cell>D</cell>
                <cell> <fillin answer="D" mode="string" /></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Grade F</cell>
                <cell>0</cell>
                <cell>F</cell>
                <cell> <fillin answer="F" mode="string" /></cell>
            </row>
